A||N||5||Dan87GT||V6 Still overheating||||1||
Z||000000||Dan87GT||11-27-2001||10:04 AM||||I hust installed a new engine in my 86SE and it keeps overheating when the thermostat is installed. Take it out and it is fine. I have tried 3 different thermostats, drilled small holes in a thermostat, slightly jacked up the rear and performed the proper coolant refill process. Any ideas? I have to run one up here in Erie, PA because I will freeze in the winter.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||11-27-2001||10:18 AM||||How "New" is the engine? Gargoyle had a simiular problem then it got worse. Found out the water pump had a plastic impeller on it. Once the system pressurized, the impeller would slip on the shaft. We replaced it with a steel impeller, high flow water pump. It's been fine since.<BR>If there is no air in the system then the water pump would be my guess. You can test the thermostat in a pot of boiling water. Boil the water, put the thermostat in and see if it opens.<P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||avengador1||11-27-2001||10:31 AM||||Don't forget to use a thermometer ( I think a meat thermometer will work) to see at what temp the thermostat opens. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||11-27-2001||12:12 PM||||More things to check:<BR>1. Coolant mixture should be 60% anti-freeze to 40% water for maximum cooling and freeze protection.<P>2. Try to look in at the condition of the radiator tubes (inside). They may have a coating on them preventing good heat transfer.<P>3. Check ignition timing, set wrong it can make the engine run hot. Also it has been known that the timing degree indicators (which are supposed to be set at true TDC when they rebuild the engine), can come loose, or are set wrong (they are position adjustable with in set screw).<P>4. Check the drive belt for tension.<P>Hopefully you know the history of the car, use common sense, go after the easy most likely things first.<P>Good Luck ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fformula88||11-27-2001||12:33 PM||||Thats odd that it will overheat with a stat, but not without.  Sounds like with the stat in it there isn't enough pressure to circulate the coolant enough.  I agree with checking the waterpump!<P>If it were a blockage, I would imagine it would do it all the time.  Coolant mixture, even if it was pure antifreeze, shouldn't cause it to overheat with the cool temps we have had in the Northeast lately.  <P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dan87GT||11-27-2001||12:48 PM||||I am stumped too..I also put in a 200/210 fan switch. It will sit there all day @ 210 without a thermostat. I totally rebuilt the motor in Sept. and only have about 150 miles on it. I replaced the water pump and it does have a metal impeller. I'll check the belt. ||||reg||1
